ebook handled missing values by first identifying the number of NaN values. Then, the notebook removes all columns containing NaN values with the pandas drop() and dropna() function. \n\n## 2.2. Splitting the Dataset into Split & Train\nThe notebook splits the dataset into training and testing variable pairs using the train_test_split() function. The 'test_size' value determines the allocation of data. Thus, 0.2, or 20%, of the data is used for testing, leaving 80% for training. The random_state value is 42 so that every run outputs the same prediction accuracy.\n\n# 3. Model Implementation\nThe notebook validates the initial train_test_split set using the K-Fold cross validation method. This method is effective and reliable for validating a model performance on a multivariate dataset instead of predicting on a random, lucky or unlucky split produced from a single split using train_test_split.\nFirst, the data is split into *K* folds. In each split, the validation subset progresses one fold at a time, leaving the remaining folds for the training set. Thus, the model trains on the training set and predicts on the validation set. The model's performance is evaluated by calculating the Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Mean Squared Error (MSE), and R² score. The result is the evaluation metrics per split and the Cross Validation (CV) summary, showing the respective averages of all evaluation scores. \n\n# 4. Results\nThe model predicts two estimations for each sample's Heating Load and Cooling Load based on the 8 building features. \nAn average MAE of 2.19 indicates that the model's predictions are off by 2.19 kWh/m² on average. \nAn average MSE of 9.52 indicates that the model's performance may not be reliable for highly accurate energy predictions. However, this score is expected for standard linear regression. \nLastly, an R² score of 0.89 indicates that the model's performance is strong, predicting energy efficiency with minimal errors. \n\n# 5. Interpretation\nThe **Actual vs. Predicted Values** scatter plots illustrate how well the model's performance by showing the distance between the predictions and the actual values. Comparing the two graphs, the model predicted Heating Load more accurately than Cooling Load. This is due to the close clustering of points along the 'Perfect Prediction' line, indicating that the predictions closely match the actual values.\nThe **Error Distribution** histograms support this relationship, as the Heating Load residuals display a roughly normal, unbiased distribution centered around zero. In contrast, the Cooling Load residuals exhibit a mild right skew caused by a few outliers, indicating occasional underprediction on extreme cases.","metadata":{}}]}